<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Aru Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">1225 CE – 1613 CE (approx.) · capital Kota Rentang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Karo and Malay kingdom on the east coast of North Sumatra, rival of Malacca and Aceh for control of the Strait of Malacca.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Haru appears in Chinese records from the 13th century and in the Javanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagarakretagama">Nagarakretagama</a> of 1365. Its centre lay near modern Medan, where the sites of Kota Rentang and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-cina">Kota Cina</a> have yielded Chinese ceramics and coins and South Indian-style Buddhist and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> statues from an earlier trading settlement.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Aru drew on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karo-batak">Karo Batak</a> peoples of the interior and on Malay coastal trade, exporting forest products, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camphor">camphor</a> and horses. Ming envoys visited, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-he">Zheng He's</a> fleets called in the early 15th century. Its rulers adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, though Malay sources such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-annals">Malay Annals</a> describe the people as raiders who challenged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> at sea.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> fell to the Portuguese in 1511, Aru was caught between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/portuguese-malacca">Portuguese Malacca</a>, the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aceh">Aceh</a> attacked repeatedly and finally conquered it in 1613 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iskandar-muda">Iskandar Muda</a>. Its lands later formed the core of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/deli-sultanate">Deli Sultanate</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/aru-kingdom.jpg" alt="Aru Kingdom" width="320" height="251"><figcaption>Image: Giovanni Battista Ramusio or Giacomo Gastaldi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1565_Sumatra_Ramusio_Delle_Navigationi_vol3_pp433-434.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1225 CE – 1613 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kota Rentang</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam, earlier Hinduism and Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Malay, Karo</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Haru, Kingdom of Aru</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1419&amp;focus=aru-kingdom" aria-label="Aru Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7510 2213 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Aru Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7642 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7830 2469h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7830 2469h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Indonesia. <a href="/atlas?year=1419&amp;focus=aru-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

